{"name":"Equator Glow","hex":"#ffe6a0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"equator","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An imaginary great circle around Earth, equidistant from the two poles, and dividing earth's surface into the northern and southern hemispheres.","A similar great circle on any sphere, especially on a celestial body, or on another reasonably symmetrical three-dimensional body."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/equator"},{"term":"glow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To emit heat and light without a flame.","To emit heat and light without a flame."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of heat and light being emitted by a hot object.","A state of heat being emitted by a person or an animal's body."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Equator","blocks":[{"text":"The equator is the circle of latitude that divides Earth into the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. It is an imaginary line located at 0 degrees latitude, about 21,639 nautical miles in circumference, located halfway between the North and South Poles. The term can also be used for any other celestial body that is roughly spherical."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Solstice_revolving_earth.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equator"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/Equator_monument.jpg/500px-Equator_monument.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/Equator_monument.jpg/500px-Equator_monument.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEquator_monument.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Equator_sign_kenya.jpg/500px-Eq